A||N||7||fierovette||Hood flys open||||5||
Z||000000||fierovette||07-23-2001||12:39 PM||||I took off my wing, only problem is when I hit the release, the hood flys open. Is there an adjustment on the hood to compensate for the loss of weight by removing the wing?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Tigger||07-23-2001||12:55 PM||||There were different decklid torsion springs to compensate for the weight of the wing.  You can swap a spring from a non-wing factory car.  I haven't did this job, search the technical part of the forum for info on how to do it.  I've heard the spring will snap back and break the rear window if you're not careful.  Another alternative would be to make a rear strut system or buy one from <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com" TARGET=_blank>www.rodneydickman.com</A> <P>Until then, don't let the decklid swing up on it's own.  I've seen a couple decklids that have broke near the hinge.<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 07-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Leadfoot||07-23-2001||02:12 PM||||Hey fierovette, if you want I'll trade you springs.  I have a bare decklid which I want to replace with a GT decklid, all I need is a strong set of torsion springs.  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierovette||07-23-2001||02:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Leadfoot:<BR><B>Hey fierovette, if you want I'll trade you springs.  I have a bare decklid which I want to replace with a GT decklid, all I need is a strong set of torsion springs.  <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>   Im trying to have it both ways, sometimes with the wing and sometimes without, but so far I cant find any decent looking black plugs for the 4 holes, and there are oval marks from the wing gasket, and with the spring deal, I guess it just easier to put the wing back on.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Leadfoot||07-23-2001||04:18 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierovette:<BR><B> <BR>   Im trying to have it both ways, sometimes with the wing and sometimes without</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeah I know what you mean.  Some days I just love the way the wingless decklid looks....but there are times I wish I had the wing.  That's why I bought another decklid with a wing.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Wolfhound||07-23-2001||05:05 PM||||Add lead weights to the bottom side of the hood that are equal to the weight of the wing. Use the wings mounting holes and add two 5 pound weights.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fierovette||07-23-2001||05:57 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Wolfhound:<BR><B>Add lead weights to the bottom side of the hood that are equal to the weight of the wing. Use the wings mounting holes and add two 5 pound weights.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>  Yea that was my next idea, `I'll hang a couple of dunbells inside the deck!<P>||||reg||3
Z||000007||Wolfhound||07-23-2001||06:10 PM||||A friend of mine makes his own down riggers for deep sea fishing by pouring lead in a beer can. 5 pounds of lead is 1 inch deep in a beer can. It a good way to have both a wing and non wing use. I wasn't being flippant.||||reg||1
